# Paper running example (Asma et al., NPCS, WWW'24 — Example 3.2), encoded under
# RDF standard reification. Each statement node u_i is a provenance token; NPCS
# binds ?fprov to it. A base triple with two sources (u1, u2) yields (u1 ⊕ u2).
#
# Use with:  java -cp target/npcs-rewrite.jar npcs.RunExample Standard \
#              examples/data/example.standard.ttl <query.sparql>

@prefix ex:  <http://example.org/paper#> .
@prefix rdf: <http://www.w3.org/1999/02/22-rdf-syntax-ns#> .

# --- Example 3.2 (verbatim): Alice ---
ex:u1 rdf:subject ex:Alice ; rdf:predicate ex:likes   ; rdf:object ex:pasta .
ex:u2 rdf:subject ex:Alice ; rdf:predicate ex:likes   ; rdf:object ex:pasta .   # second source of the same triple
ex:u3 rdf:subject ex:Alice ; rdf:predicate ex:livesIn ; rdf:object ex:Italy .

# --- Added so UNION / MINUS / OPTIONAL return non-trivial provenance ---
ex:u4 rdf:subject ex:Bob   ; rdf:predicate ex:likes   ; rdf:object ex:pasta .   # Bob likes pasta, no known country
ex:u5 rdf:subject ex:Carol ; rdf:predicate ex:livesIn ; rdf:object ex:Italy .   # Carol lives in Italy, no known food
